:title("Ruby (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a Ruby subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset matches ruby-interpreter.abnf: local variables (create on first assignment,
plus += -= *= /= %=), the numeric tower (Integer, Float, Rational 3r, Complex 2i - all
distinct, so 7 / 2 == 3 and 7.0 / 2 == 3.5 hold at once), double/single quoted strings
with '#{expr}' interpolation, heredocs, the percent literals, true/false/nil, Symbols
(their own type, not strings), arithmetic and comparison as METHODS (a class with
def + / def <=> / def [] joins the operator table), && || ! and the words and/or/not,
def with implicit return of the last expression and explicit return, default / splat /
keyword / block parameters, yield and block_given?, lambdas and procs, class C ... end
with instance methods, def self.m class methods, @ivars, @@class variables, $globals,
C.new and inheritance with super, if/elsif/else and unless (also as expressions),
case/when (including when Integer and when *arr), while/until with break/next, ranges
1..5 / 1...5 (eager arrays) with .each, arrays with [] get/set, hashes {k => v},
blocks (do |x| ... end and { |x| ... }, real closures via js_closure) and puts/print.

Locals, functions, @ivars, methods and blocks lower to the MetaJS handle runtime:
scopes via js_scope_new / js_pyset_var (Ruby create-on-assign) / js_scope_get, closures
via js_closure + js_call, objects/@ivars via js_obj_new / js_get / js_set, method
dispatch via js_rmcall (the Ruby builtin methods on strings/arrays/hashes with Ruby
truthiness and nil-on-empty; instances carry a __class descriptor, C.new runs initialize),
arrays via js_arr_new / js_arr_push, indexing via js_rget / js_rset, hashes via
js_dict_new. The operators are Ruby's own externs (js_radd, js_rdiv, js_rcmp, ...):
they carry the numeric tower (js_rflo / js_rrat / js_rcpx box Float, Rational and
Complex), Ruby floor division, un-truncated Integers and the user operator methods. Ruby truthiness (only nil and false are falsy) is a single handle compare
(a value handle >= the 'true' singleton). The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args);
a self checking program ends with exit(failures). The same subset is interpreted
directly by ruby-interpreter.abnf, and the two must agree.") ;
// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib): identifiers, integers, booleans.
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Ruby '#' line comments (the '#{' of an interpolation lives inside a string
// token, which disables whitespace skipping, so it never reaches here).
Whitespace  = { @+" \t\r\n" | Comment | LineJoin } ;
// A backslash at the end of a line joins it to the next one, so the pair is nothing
// but whitespace (`.should == \` continued on the following line).
LineJoin    = "\\" :whitespace() "\r"..."\r" "\n"..."\n" :whitespace(Whitespace)
            | "\\" :whitespace() "\n"..."\n" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
Comment     = "#" :whitespace() { NotNewline } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
NotNewline  = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// The whole program is a sequence of definitions and statements executed in order.
Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = { BodyItem } ;
// A def / class / module is a STATEMENT in Ruby, not only a top-level item: it may
// stand inside a method, an if, a block ('it "..." do def helper; end end' is the
// shape most of a spec suite has). Body and Program therefore accept the same items.
BodyItem    = HdBodyItem | AliasTop | Statement ;

// module M ... end: accepted but not implemented (no namespacing). Its body is a
// program of the same items; under -warn-unsupported they run in the current scope
// so any defs/classes/calls stay visible. A nested module recurses.
ModuleDecl  <~~ push(makeModuleStmt(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwModule ScopedName <~~ push(up.in) ~~>
              { Semi | Def <~~ push({method: pop()}) ~~> | ClassDecl <~~ push({nested: pop(), nestedName: lastDeclName}) ~~>
              | ModuleDecl <~~ push({nested: pop(), nestedName: lastDeclName}) ~~> | AliasStmt | AliasMethodStmt
              | DefineMethodStmt | MixinStmt | VisStmt | ClassBodyStmt } [ ClassGuard ] End ;
// A constant path Foo::Bar::Baz (module / class name or a scoped reference).
ScopedName  = [ "::" ] Name { "::" Name } ;
// The name in a class/module header is normally a constant path, but Ruby also parses
// a path rooted at a keyword literal (`class nil::Foo`) and rejects it at RUN time
// with a TypeError - so it has to get past the parser first.
ClassPath   = KwNilPath | ScopedName ;
KwNilPath   = "nil" KwEnd "::" Name ;

// A def: implicit return of the last expression, or an explicit return. A singleton
// receiver (def self.m / def obj.m) is accepted but not implemented (routed as a
// plain method of that name). The parameter list accepts default / splat / double-
// splat / block / keyword parameters; the fancy forms are notImplemented (the method
// is still built with the parameter names, so its body stays compilable).
Def         <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDef [ SelfRecv ] DefName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ParamList ] DefTail ;
// The name a def may carry. SetterName comes FIRST (PEG): `def name=(v)` declares the
// assignment method `name=`, and it is told apart from the endless `def name = EXPR`
// by the `=` being GLUED to the name - which is exactly Ruby's own rule, and has to
// be a :script because a token match would skip the space in front of the `=`.
DefName     = SetterName | MethodName ;
SetterName  = Id EqGlued "=" !"=" NotTilde ;
EqGlued = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) !== 61) { return abnf.newToken("\x01noglue", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
// An endless def (def sq(x) = x * x) has an EXPRESSION body and no `end`; the
// ordinary body may carry rescue/else/ensure clauses with no `begin` of their own.
DefTail     = EndlessBody | GuardedBody ;
EndlessBody <~~ push({body: pop()}) ~~>
            = "=" !"=" Expression ;
GuardedBody <~~ push({body: makeGuardedBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = GuardedItems End ;
// The clause sequence itself, without the closing `end`, so a do-block body can reuse
// it: `[1].each do |x| ... rescue ... ensure ... end` is legal Ruby too.
GuardedItems = Body <~~ push({trybody: pop().body}) ~~> { RescueClause }
              [ KwElse Body <~~ push({elsebody: pop().body}) ~~> ]
              [ KwEnsure Body <~~ push({finalbody: pop().body}) ~~> ] ;
// def self.m: a method of the class object itself, not of its instances.
SelfRecv    <~~ push({selfrecv: true}) ~~>
            = SelfRecvHead "." ;
// `def obj.m` also names the receiver with an @ivar, a @@classvar, a $global or a
// scoped constant (`def @obj.foo`, `def Foo::Bar.m`) - all of them singleton defs.
SelfRecvHead = KwSelf | "@@" Id | "@" Id | "$" Id | ScopedName ;
ParamList   = "(" [ Param { "," Param } [ "," ] ] ")" ;
Param       = ParamFwd | ParamDSplat | ParamSplat | ParamBlock | ParamKw | ParamDefault
            | BGroup | ParamSimple ;
// def m(...) - argument forwarding. It is exactly `*__fwd_a, &__fwd_b` under two
// reserved names, and `m(...)` on the call side splices the same two back out, so no
// new binding machinery is needed at all.
ParamFwd    <~~ push({param: "__fwd_a", splat: true}) ; push({param: "__fwd_b", blockp: true}) ~~>
            = "..." ;
// `**nil` declares that the method takes no keyword arguments; it is the same shape
// as an anonymous `**` for everything the binder does.
ParamDSplat <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({param: n.length > 0 ? n[0] : null, kwrest: true}) ~~>
            = "**" [ KwNil | Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ;
KwNil       = "nil" KwEnd ;
ParamSplat  <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({param: n.length > 0 ? n[0] : null, splat: true}) ~~>
            = "*" !"*" [ Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ;
// &blk: the block the call was given, received as an ordinary named parameter.
// `def m(&)` declares an ANONYMOUS block parameter, and `f(&)` inside such a method
// forwards it. Both are the same reserved name, exactly as `...` is spelled out above.
ParamBlock  <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({param: n.length > 0 ? n[0] : "__fwd_b", blockp: true}) ~~>
            = "&" !"&" [ Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ] ;
// name: / name: DEFAULT - a keyword parameter, filled from the call's trailing hash.
ParamKw     <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({param: n[0], kw: true, defT: n.length > 1 ? n[1] : undefined}) ~~>
            = Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":" NotColonGlued [ Expression ] ;
ParamDefault <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({param: n[0], defT: n[1]}) ~~>
            = Name <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "=" !"=" Expression ;
ParamSimple = Name <~~ push({param: up.in}) ~~> ;
// Method / call names may end with ? or ! (empty?, include?); Name may not. A def
// may equally name an OPERATOR (def +, def -@, def [], def []=, def <=>), which is
// how a class joins the operator table - the longest spelling comes first (PEG).
MethodName  = OpMethodName | Id [ "?" | "!" NotEqGlued ] ;
// `A.new.should != 2` - without this the `!` is taken as part of the method name and
// the `= 2` is left over. It has to be a :script rather than a `!"="` lookahead,
// because a lookahead skips the space in front of it and would then also reject the
// perfectly good `x.foo! == 1`.
NotEqGlued = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 61) { return abnf.newToken("\x01eq", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
OpMethodName = "[]=" | "[]" | "<=>" | "===" | "==" | "!=" | "<<" | ">>" | "<=" | ">="
            | "**" | "+@" | "-@" | "=~" | "+" | "-" | "*" | "/" | "%" | "<" | ">"
            | "&" | "|" | "^" | "~" | "!" | "`" ;

// class C ... end with instance methods; C.new builds an instance. attr_reader /
// attr_writer / attr_accessor declare @ivar getters/setters on the class.
// A superclass (class D < Base) is accepted but not implemented (no inheritance);
// the class body still compiles as its own methods.
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwClass ClassPath <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ Superclass ]
              { Semi | SingletonBlock | Def <~~ push({method: pop()}) ~~> | AttrDecl
              | AliasStmt | AliasMethodStmt | DefineMethodStmt | MixinStmt | VisStmt
              | ClassDecl <~~ push({nested: pop(), nestedName: lastDeclName}) ~~>
              | ModuleDecl <~~ push({nested: pop(), nestedName: lastDeclName}) ~~>
              | ClassBodyStmt } [ ClassGuard ] End ;
// A class body may carry its own rescue / else / ensure clauses, with no `begin` of
// its own - exactly like a def body. They come after the body items, and no class-body
// item can start with `rescue` or `ensure`, so the item loop stops there by itself.
ClassGuard  <~~ push({cguard: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = RescueClause { RescueClause } [ KwElse Body <~~ push({elsebody: pop().body}) ~~> ]
              [ KwEnsure Body <~~ push({finalbody: pop().body}) ~~> ]
            | KwEnsure Body <~~ push({finalbody: pop().body}) ~~> ;

// class << self ... end inside a class body: every def inside it is a method of the
// class object. Written as its own alternative of the class body so the defs can be
// folded straight into the enclosing class rather than compiled as a nested statement.
SingletonBlock <~~ push({sblock: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwClass "<<" KwSelf SgItems [ ClassGuard ] End ;
// class << EXPR ... end anywhere else: the singleton ("eigen") class of a VALUE.
// `class << obj`, `class << true`, `class << CONST` all open it. We approximate the
// eigenclass by the value's own class object, which is what the specs that ask for
// `class << x; self; end` actually compare against, and a def inside it becomes a
// singleton method when the value IS a class (the `class << self` case) and an
// ordinary method of its class otherwise. It is a PRIMARY and not only a statement:
// `class << true; self; end.should == TrueClass` takes a method suffix on the `end`.
SingletonDecl <~~ push(makeSingletonStmt(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwClass "<<" SgTarget SgItems [ ClassGuard ] End ;
SgTarget    = KwSelf <~~ push({sgself: true}) ~~> | Expression <~~ push({sgexpr: pop()}) ~~> ;
SgItems     = { Semi | Def <~~ push({method: pop()}) ~~> | AttrDecl | AliasStmt
            | ClassDecl <~~ push({cstmt: pop()}) ~~> | ModuleDecl <~~ push({cstmt: pop()}) ~~>
            | ClassBodyStmt } ;
// `alias' outside a class body - at top level or inside a block (`class_eval do
// alias new old end'). The enclosing class is only known at run time there, so this
// is the statement wrapper around the same declaration.
AliasTop    <~~ push(makeAliasTop(pop().alias, up.pos)) ~~>
            = AliasStmtDyn ;
// Outside a class body the alias names may be COMPUTED: `alias :"#{name}" value` is
// ordinary Ruby. A class body's alias stays on the static AliasStmt above, because the
// method tables it feeds are built while walking.
AliasStmtDyn <~~ push({alias: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwAlias AliasNameDyn AliasNameDyn ;
AliasNameDyn = AliasDqInterp | AliasName ;
AliasDqInterp = ":" ColonGlued DqStr ;
// alias NEW OLD (a keyword, unlike alias_method which is an ordinary call).
AliasStmt   <~~ push({alias: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = KwAlias AliasName AliasName ;
// An alias names a method as a bare word, as a symbol (:name, :"name", :'name') or -
// for the global-variable form of alias - as a $global.
AliasName   = AliasSq | AliasDq | ":" :whitespace() SymName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace)
            | AliasGvarP | AliasGvar | MethodName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;
AliasSq     = ":" :whitespace() "'" SymName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
AliasDq     = ":" :whitespace() '"' SymName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
AliasGvarP  = "$" :whitespace() GvarNumCh <~~ push("$" + up.in) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
AliasGvar   = "$" Id <~~ push("$" + up.in) ~~> ;
// The other class/module body declarations that RECONFIGURE the type rather than
// run as a statement. They are productions of their own (and not ClassBodyStmt
// calls) because makeClassStmt has to see them IN ORDER: a `private` on a line of
// its own applies to every def after it.
AliasMethodStmt <~~ push({alias: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "alias_method" KwEnd AliasName "," AliasName ;
// define_method(:name) { |x| ... }: the block is the METHOD BODY, so its params and
// body are taken apart here and rebuilt as an ordinary method descriptor - emitting
// the block as a closure instead would give it the block calling convention, and the
// receiver would arrive where the first parameter belongs.
DefineMethodStmt <~~ push(makeDefineMethod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "define_method" KwEnd DmName DmBody ;
DmBody      = KwDo [ BlockParams ] BlockBody End
            | "{" [ BlockParams ] BlockBody "}" ;
DmName      = "(" DmSym ")" | DmSym ;
DmSym       = ":" SymName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;
// include M / extend M / prepend M.
MixinStmt   <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({mixkind: n[0], mixname: n[1]}) ~~>
            = MixKind MixName ;
MixKind     <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = MixWord KwEnd ;
MixWord     = "include" | "extend" | "prepend" ;
MixName     <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = ScopedName ;
// A visibility keyword on a line of ITS OWN (the `private :name` and `private def`
// argument forms are a different construct and stay ordinary statements), which is
// what LineEnd checks.
VisStmt     <~~ push({vis: up.in}) ~~> = VisWord KwEnd LineEnd ;
VisWord     = "private" | "protected" | "public" | "module_function" ;
// class D < Base: real inheritance (method lookup walks __super, and `super` calls
// the overridden method).
Superclass  = "<" !"<" KwSelf <~~ push({supername: "self"}) ~~>
            | "<" !"<" ScopedName <~~ push({supername: up.in}) ~~> HeaderEnd
            | "<" !"<" SuperExprDrop ;
// HeaderEnd is what tells `class D < Base` from `class D < Module.new`: only a name
// that ENDS the header line is the constant path the method tables are built from;
// anything else is an expression and falls to SuperExprDrop below.
HeaderEnd = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 1000 && (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13)) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); steps = steps + 1 }
        if (cc === 10 || cc === 59 || cc === 35 || cc < 0) { return undefined }
        return abnf.newToken("\x01notheaderend", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
// Ruby evaluates the superclass, so it may be any expression - `class C < ""` parses
// and raises a TypeError at RUN time. Only a constant path names a class the method
// tables can be built from, so anything else is parsed and dropped.
SuperExprDrop <~~ pop() ~~> = PostfixExpr ;
// Anything else in a class body - @@classvar = v, include M, private, alias - runs
// as an ordinary statement with `self` bound to the class object.
ClassBodyStmt <~~ push({cstmt: pop(), ctext: up.in}) ~~>
            = Statement ;
AttrDecl    <~~ push(makeAttr(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AttrKind <~~ push({akind: up.in}) ~~> AttrSym { "," AttrSym } ;
AttrKind    = ( "attr_accessor" | "attr_reader" | "attr_writer" ) KwEnd ;
AttrSym     = ":" SymName <~~ push({aname: up.in}) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

End         = "end" KwEnd ;

// Body collects its statements into a single marker so the enclosing production
// (if/while/def/block) sees exactly one item on the stack for its body.
Body        <~~ push({body: makeBody(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = { BodyItem } ;

// Every statement pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value | signal.
// A statement may be a post-condition loop (begin..end while/until COND, runs its
// body at least once) or carry a trailing modifier (STMT if/unless/while/until
// COND). A modifier only binds when its keyword sits on the SAME source line as
// the statement (SameLine), so a block 'if'/'while' that opens the next line stays
// a separate statement.
// A bare ';' is an empty statement: Ruby uses it as a statement separator, and it
// appears immediately after a def / class / module header (def f(x); BODY; end).
// Semi pushes nothing, so a body simply skips it.
Statement   = Semi | Require | DoWhileStmt | ModStmt ;
Semi        = ";" ;
DoWhileStmt <~~ push(makeDoWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwBegin Body End SameLine DoWhileKw Expression ;
DoWhileKw   = KwWhile <~~ push({dw: "while"}) ~~> | KwUntil <~~ push({dw: "until"}) ~~> ;
ModStmt     <~~ push(applyMod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = SimpleStmt [ SameLine ModKw ModCond ] ;
// `remove_method :m if method_defined? :m` - the condition of a modifier is a full
// statement-level expression, so a paren-less call is one of the forms it may take.
ModCond     = CommandForm | Expression ;
SimpleStmt  = PutsStmt | MultiAssign | AppendStmt | CommandStmt | PatAssign | ExprStmt ;
// EXPR => PATTERN: Ruby 3's rightward assignment / destructuring statement. It raises
// when the pattern does not match, so it is a statement and not a test. FatArrowAhead
// is a cheap fast-fail: without it EVERY statement would parse its expression twice.
PatAssign   <~~ push(makePatAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = FatArrowAhead Expression "=>" PatTop [ ";" ] ;
// NAME arg[, arg] [block]: the parenthesis-less command call (`describe "x" do .. end`,
// `include Comparable`, `alias_method :other, :real`). It lives at STATEMENT level
// only: inside an argument list `f(double, 21)` a bare name is a VALUE, not a call of
// the following arguments, and Ruby itself resolves that with the local-variable
// table a PEG parser does not have. CmdArgAhead is the spacing rule Ruby uses to tell
// `foo -1` (an argument) from `foo - 1` (a subtraction).
CommandStmt = CommandForm ;
// The same form where a VALUE is expected: `code = fixture __FILE__, "x.rb"` and
// `return get_ary 3 do |i| ... end` are both paren-less calls in expression position.
// CmdArgAhead is what keeps it out of everything else - a bare name followed by a
// newline, an operator, a comma or a `)` never reaches it.
CommandForm <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BareName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> SameLine CmdArgAhead Arg { "," Arg } [ Block ] ;
// TARGET << V [<< V]: Ruby strings are mutable, so `s << "bc"` has to update the
// variable itself. The append is therefore a statement form that WRITES BACK to its
// target - for an array that is the same object it already mutated in place, for a
// string it is the new value.
AppendStmt  <~~ push(makeAppendStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target "<<" !"<" NotHeredoc AddExpr { "<<" NotHeredoc AddExpr } ;
ModKw       = KwIf <~~ push({mod: "if"}) ~~> | KwUnless <~~ push({mod: "unless"}) ~~>
            | KwWhile <~~ push({mod: "while"}) ~~> | KwUntil <~~ push({mod: "until"}) ~~> ;

// require / require_relative <string>: Ruby has no import statement - require and
// require_relative are ordinary method calls, intercepted here. The single argument is
// a single- or double-quoted string literal (no interpolation); a leading './' and a
// missing '.rb' are handled by core.importFile. Tried as the FIRST statement form so a
// bareword 'require'/'require_relative' (both ordinary identifiers, not keywords) is not
// swallowed by a plain VarRef; a 'require' not followed by a string rolls back to the
// normal statement forms. resolveImport runs at PARSE time (like every other language's
// import): the required file is compiled with importDepth > 0, so its top-level thunks are
// stashed (buildMain) and prepended to jsmain, existing before the requiring file's code.
// A require that resolves to no project file is a stdlib require: a no-op when the name is
// in core.stdlibImports, otherwise it aborts (or warns under -warn-imports). Require pushes
// no thunk (the work is done at parse time), so buildMain never sees it.
Require     <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwRequire ReqPath ;
KwRequire   = ( "require_relative" | "require" ) KwEnd ;
ReqPath     = SqPath | DqPath ;
SqPath      = "'" :whitespace() { SqEsc | SqPlain } <~~ push(unescapeSq(up.in)) ~~> "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
DqPath      = '"' :whitespace() { DqEsc | DqPathChar } <~~ push(unescapeJs(up.in)) ~~> '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
DqEsc       = "\\" "\x20"..b"\x7e" ;
DqPathChar  = "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" | @"\t" ;

// a, b = 1, 2 (and the swap a, b = b, a): every right-hand value is evaluated
// before any target is written; a single right-hand array is unpacked positionally.
// Kept at statement level (needs a comma after the first target) so it never
// interferes with a nested expression.
MultiAssign <~~ push(makeMultiAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MSplat "=" !"=" RetVal { "," RetVal } [ ";" ]
            | MGroup "=" !"=" RetVal { "," RetVal } [ ";" ]
            | MTarget "=" !"=" NotTilde RetVal "," RetVal { "," RetVal } [ ";" ]
            | MTarget "," [ MTarget { "," MTarget } [ "," ] ] "=" RetVal { "," RetVal } [ ";" ] ;
// A multiple-assignment target: a splat target (*rest, which collects the leftover
// values into an array), a parenthesized nested group ((m, n), o = [5, 6], 7) or an
// ordinary target. Longest first, so *x and (x, y) are not read as a plain Target.
MTarget     = MSplat | MGroup | Target ;
// The name may be omitted: `*, last = arr` and `(* = 1)` discard what they collect.
MSplat      <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({splat: n.length > 0 ? n[0] : makeTargetRef([{var: "__splat_discard"}])}) ~~>
            = "*" !"*" [ Target ] ;
MGroup      <~~ push({group: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" MTarget [ "," [ MTarget { "," MTarget } ] [ "," ] ] ")" ;

// puts / print command form: puts x, puts a, b, puts (no args), puts(x).
PutsStmt    <~~ push(makePuts(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwPuts <~~ push({cmd: up.in}) ~~> [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ;

ExprStmt    = Expression [ ";" ] ;

// Every expression pushes exactly one thunk: function() -> value. A ternary
// cond ? a : b binds looser than the boolean operators (like Ruby).
// The word forms and/or/not bind LOOSER than assignment in Ruby, which is the whole
// point of `x = false or true` (x is false, the `or true` is a separate step). They
// therefore sit ABOVE the assignment, in their own three levels, while the symbol
// forms && || ! keep their tight precedence inside OrExpr/AndExpr/NotExpr below.
Expression  = LowOr ;
LowOr       <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = LowAnd { OrWord LowAnd } ;
LowAnd      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = LowNot { AndWord LowNot } ;
LowNot      = LowNotOp | PatTest ;
// EXPR in PATTERN is a boolean test (Ruby 3.0). SameLine is what keeps the `in` of a
// `case SUBJ` newline `in ...' clause from being read as a test on the subject.
PatTest     <~~ push(makePatTest(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RescueMod [ SameLine KwIn Pattern ] ;
// EXPR rescue FALLBACK: the expression modifier form of rescue. SameLine keeps it
// clear of a def-level `rescue` clause that opens the next line.
RescueMod   <~~ push(makeRescueMod(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AssignExpr [ SameLine KwRescue AssignExpr ] ;
LowNotOp    <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = NotWord LowNot ;
OrWord      = "or" KwEnd ;
AndWord     = "and" KwEnd ;
NotWord     = "not" KwEnd ;

AssignExpr  = Assignment | Ternary ;
Ternary     <~~ push(makeTernary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OrExpr [ "?" Expression ":" Expression ] ;

Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Target AssignOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> AssignRhs ;
// `x = *arr` copies the array (and wraps a non-array in one); `x = f a, b` is a
// paren-less call. Both are tried before the ordinary expression, and both fail on
// the very first token when the right side is anything else.
AssignRhs   = SplatRhs | CommandForm | AssignExpr ;
SplatRhs    <~~ push(makeSplatRhs(pop())) ~~>
            = "*" !"*" AssignExpr ;
// ||= and &&= (conditional assignment) are accepted but not implemented (no short
// circuit); makeAssign warns and falls back to a plain assignment.
// NotTilde is what keeps `s =~ /re/` out of the assignment path: `=` followed by
// `~` is the match operator, not an assignment of a bitwise-not. It has to be a
// :script rather than a `!"~"` lookahead, because a lookahead SKIPS the whitespace
// in front of it and would then also reject the perfectly good `x = ~5`.
AssignOp    = "||=" | "&&=" | "**=" | "<<=" | ">>=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%="
            | "|=" | "&=" | "^=" | ( "=" !"=" NotTilde ) ;
// A label's `:` may not be the first half of a `::`. It has to be a :script anchored
// on the very next byte, because a `!":"` lookahead SKIPS the whitespace in front of
// it - which made `{ |a, b: :b| ... }` reject its own keyword parameter, the `:` of
// the symbol VALUE being what the lookahead found. Same trap as `!"literal"` above.
NotColonGlued = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 58) { return abnf.newToken("\x01coloncolon", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
NotTilde = :script(~~
    (function() {
        if (c.peek(0) === 126) { return abnf.newToken("\x01tilde", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;
Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TargetHead { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | "&." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~>
              | "::" Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
// The '@@' alternative must come BEFORE '@' (PEG: the shorter one would win), and the
// scoped-constant head before the plain name for the same reason. `self` is a head of
// its own: `self.attr = v` is how a method writes through its own accessor.
TargetHead  = "$" :whitespace() GvarDashCh <~~ push({gvar: up.in}) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace)
            | ParenTarget
            | "::" Name <~~ push({var: up.in}) ~~>
            | "$" :whitespace() GvarNumCh <~~ push({gvar: up.in}) ~~> :whitespace(Whitespace)
            | "$" Id <~~ push({gvar: up.in}) ~~>
            | "@@" Id <~~ push({cvar: up.in}) ~~>
            | "@" Id <~~ push({ivar: up.in}) ~~>
            | KwSelf <~~ push({var: "self"}) ~~>
            | ScopedConstName <~~ push({sconst: up.in}) ~~>
            | Name <~~ push({var: up.in}) ~~> ;
ScopedConstName = Name "::" Name { "::" Name } ;
// A parenthesised statement sequence used as an assignment TARGET:
// `(ScratchPad << :x; obj).a = 1`. It is guarded, because Target is attempted at every
// assignment level and parsing a whole statement list inside every parenthesised
// expression would be a 2^depth blow-up on nested parentheses. The guard walks to the
// MATCHING close paren and only lets the alternative run when a `.` or `[` follows it,
// which is the only shape where a parenthesised head can be a target at all.
ParenTarget <~~ push({texpr: pop().body}) ~~>
            = ParenTargetAhead "(" Body ")" ;
ParenTargetAhead = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var no = abnf.newToken("\x01noparentarget", 0)
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            if (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 13 || cc === 10) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i); continue }
            if (cc === 35) { while (cc !== 10 && cc !== -1) { i = i + 1; cc = c.peek(i) } continue }
            break
        }
        if (cc !== 40) { return no }
        var depth = 0
        var guard = 0
        while (guard < 100000) {
            guard = guard + 1
            var d = c.peek(i)
            if (d < 0) { return no }
            if (d === 34 || d === 39) {                    // step over a string
                var q = d
                i = i + 1
                var e = c.peek(i)
                while (e >= 0 && e !== q) {
                    if (e === 92) { i = i + 1 }
                    i = i + 1
                    e = c.peek(i)
                }
                if (e !== q) { return no }
                i = i + 1
                continue
            }
            if (d === 40 || d === 91 || d === 123) { depth = depth + 1; i = i + 1; continue }
            if (d === 41 || d === 93 || d === 125) {
                depth = depth - 1
                i = i + 1
                if (depth === 0) { break }
                continue
            }
            i = i + 1
        }
        if (depth !== 0) { return no }
        var j = i
        var f = c.peek(j)
        var st2 = 0
        while (st2 < 1000 && (f === 32 || f === 9 || f === 13)) { j = j + 1; f = c.peek(j); st2 = st2 + 1 }
        if (f === 46 || f === 91) { return undefined }     // '.' or '['
        if (f === 58 && c.peek(j + 1) === 58) { return undefined }   // '::'
        if (f === 38 && c.peek(j + 1) === 46) { return undefined }   // '&.'
        return no
    })()
~~) ;

// A subscript target may carry SEVERAL arguments (`@obj[1, *ary] = 4` reaches a
// user-defined `[]=` with three). Only the first is lowered; the rest are parsed so
// the surrounding file is understood, which is what a multi-key []= costs here.
TIndex      <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({idx: n[0]}) ~~>
            = "[" Arg { "," Arg } "]" ;

// The RIGHT operand of && / || may be an ASSIGNMENT: `true && false && x = 1` and
// `x = 1 || false && x = 2` are ordinary Ruby, because `=` binds looser than the
// boolean operators and so can only appear on their right. Assignment is tried first
// (PEG) and fails cheaply - Target rejects `false`, a literal or a call at once.
OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { OrOp OrRhs } ;
OrRhs       = Assignment | AndExpr ;
OrOp        = "||" ;
AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = NotExpr { AndOp AndRhs } ;
AndRhs      = Assignment | NotExpr ;
AndOp       = "&&" ;
NotExpr     = NotOp | EqExpr ;
NotOp       <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "!" !"=" NotExpr ;

// =~ and !~ are the regexp match operators; they sit at the equality level (Ruby),
// and either operand may be the pattern.
EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "===" | "==" | "=~" | "!=" | "!~" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;
// <=> (the spaceship) is listed FIRST so "<=" cannot swallow its prefix.
RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitOrExpr { ( "<=>" | "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitOrExpr } ;
// Bitwise | ^ and & (the lookaheads keep || && and the safe-navigation &. out).
BitOrExpr   <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = BitAndExpr { BitOrOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> BitAndExpr } ;
BitOrOp     = ( "|" !"|" ) | "^" ;
BitAndExpr  <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RangeExpr { BitAndOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RangeExpr } ;
BitAndOp    = "&" !"&" !"." ;
// A range 1..5 (inclusive) or 1...5 (exclusive), and the endless (5..) / beginless
// (..9) forms; built as a lazy Range object.
// The beginless form comes FIRST (PEG): it starts with the operator, so nothing else
// can match it, and makeRange recognizes it by items[0] being the operator string.
RangeExpr   <~~ push(makeRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RangeOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ShiftExpr
            | ShiftExpr [ RangeOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ShiftExpr ] ] ;
RangeOp     = "..." | ".." ;
// The << and >> operators: on an array << appends (and yields the array, so
// a << b << c chains), on a string it concatenates, on an integer it shifts.
ShiftExpr   <~~ push(makeShift(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { ShiftOp <~~ push(up.in) ~~> NotHeredoc AddExpr } ;
ShiftOp     = "<<" | ">>" ;
// A `<<` that is GLUED to a heredoc marker opens a heredoc, not a shift/append:
// `f <<-A do` is a command call whose argument is a heredoc, and without this guard
// AppendStmt reads it as `f << (-A)` - which SUCCEEDS, so there is no parse error to
// point at, only a body that turns up later as unparseable code.
NotHeredoc = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var f = c.peek(i)
        var dec = false
        if (f === 126 || f === 45) { dec = true; i = i + 1; f = c.peek(i) }
        var qt = 0
        if (f === 39 || f === 34) { qt = f; i = i + 1; f = c.peek(i) }
        var nameOk = (f >= 65 && f <= 90) || (f >= 97 && f <= 122) || f === 95
        var up0 = (f >= 65 && f <= 90) || f === 95
        if (nameOk && (dec || qt !== 0 || up0)) { return abnf.newToken("\x01heredoc", 0) }
    })()
~~) ;

// The LineCont guard is what keeps `first, *rest = [1, 2, 3]' followed by a line
// that OPENS with `*init, last = ...' from reading as one multiplication: in Ruby a
// newline ends the statement once the expression is complete, so a binary operator
// that starts a fresh source line never continues the previous expression.
AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { LineCont ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;
MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { LineCont ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

UnaryExpr   = Neg | Pos | BitNot | PowExpr ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>
            = "-" UnaryExpr ;
// Unary plus. On a number it is the identity; Ruby's real use is `+"str"`, which asks
// for an unfrozen copy of a string literal - and a copy is what the value already is
// here, so the operand is simply handed back.
Pos         <~~ push(makePos(pop())) ~~>
            = "+" UnaryExpr ;
BitNot      <~~ push(makeBitNot(pop())) ~~>
            = "~" UnaryExpr ;
// ** binds tighter than unary minus (-2 ** 2 is -4) and is RIGHT associative
// (2 ** 3 ** 2 is 512), so its right operand is a whole UnaryExpr again.
PowExpr     <~~ push(makePow(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PostfixExpr [ "**" UnaryExpr ] ;

// obj.method(args), obj.method do |x| ... end, obj.field, arr[i]. A trailing
// block (do..end or {..}) becomes the last argument of the method call.
PostfixExpr <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { CallSfx | CmdMethodSfx | MethodSfx | FieldSfx | SafeMethodSfx | SafeFieldSfx | IndexSfx } ;
// obj.m a, b - the parenthesis-less call WITH a receiver (`ScratchPad.record []`,
// `self.class.send :define_method, m do |x| ... end`, `a.should_receive :m`). It has
// to be a SUFFIX rather than a statement form of its own, because the suffix loop is
// greedy and would already have taken the `.m` as a field access by the time a
// statement-level rule could ask for it. CmdArgAhead is the same spacing rule the
// receiver-less CommandStmt uses, so `a.b - 1`, `x ? a.b : c` and `a.b { }` are not
// mistaken for argument lists; it comes BEFORE MethodSfx so the parenthesised form
// still wins whenever there is a `(`.
CmdMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." MethodName <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> SameLine CmdArgAhead Arg { "," Arg } [ Block ] ;
MethodSfx   <~~ push({kind: "mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." MethodName <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> MArgs ;
// p.(x): the parenthesis-only call shorthand for a Proc (Proc#call).
CallSfx     <~~ push({kind: "mcall", name: "call", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." "(" [ Arg { "," Arg } ] ")" ;
FieldSfx    = "." MethodName <~~ push({kind: "field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
// Safe navigation obj&.m / obj&.m(args): yields nil (and skips the call, not even
// evaluating the arguments) when the receiver is nil, else a normal method/field.
SafeMethodSfx <~~ push({kind: "smcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "&." MethodName <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> MArgs ;
SafeFieldSfx = "&." MethodName <~~ push({kind: "sfield", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
// The LineCont guard keeps a line that OPENS with an array literal from being read
// as an index of the previous expression (`txt = ""' then `[[1, 2]].each ...').
// A subscript reaches a user-defined #[] with a full argument list; only the first
// argument is lowered, the rest are parsed so the surrounding file is understood.
IndexSfx    <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({kind: "index", idx: n[0]}) ~~>
            = LineCont "[" Arg { "," Arg } "]" ;
// SameLine is what stops an argument list from reaching across a line break: after
// `n.to_s` a `(` that OPENS the next line is a fresh statement, not this call's
// arguments. (Ruby wants the `(` glued to the name; requiring only the same line keeps
// the `foo (1)` spelling working, which Ruby warns about but accepts.)
MArgs       = SameLine "(" [ Arg { "," Arg } [ "," ] ] ")" [ Block ] | Block ;

// A call argument: an ordinary expression, or one of the forms accepted for
// recognition but not implemented - keyword argument (name: v), splat / double-splat
// (*a, **h) and block pass (&blk, &:sym). The unsupported forms warn and become a
// placeholder argument, so the call still compiles.
// FwdArg comes LAST: a leading "..." is first offered to Expression, which reads it
// as a beginless exclusive range and only fails when no operand follows - so `f(...)`
// forwards while `f(...9)` stays a range.
Arg         = KwArg | SplatArg | BlockArg | ArrowArg | FwdArg ;
// KEY => VALUE among the arguments: Ruby collects the trailing run of `name: v` and
// `key => v` pairs into ONE Hash and passes it as the last argument, so
// `m(kw: 1, true => false)` is a call with a single Hash. Spelled as an OPTIONAL tail
// of the ordinary argument, so a plain argument still parses its expression once.
ArrowArg    <~~ var n = takeAll(); if (n.length > 1) { push({kwkeyT: n[0], kwT: n[1]}) } else { push(n[0]) } ~~>
            = Expression [ "=>" Expression ] ;
FwdArg      <~~ push({splatT: makeVarRef("__fwd_a")}) ; push({blkT: makeVarRef("__fwd_b")}) ~~>
            = "..." ;
// The value may be OMITTED (Ruby 3.1): `m(a:, b:)` passes the local - or the method -
// of the same name, exactly like the `{x:, y:}` hash shorthand.
KwArg       <~~ var n = takeAll(); push({kwname: n[0], kwT: n.length > 1 ? n[1] : makeVarRef(n[0])}) ~~>
            = SymName <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":" NotColonGlued [ Expression ] ;
// *arr splices an array into the positional arguments, **h merges a hash into the
// keyword arguments.
SplatArg    <~~ push({splatT: pop(), dsplat: up.in.slice(0, 2) == "**"}) ~~>
            = ( "**" | "*" ) Expression ;
// &expr: pass expr as the call's block. A Proc is passed straight through; a symbol
// becomes the "call this method on each element" proc (`[1, 2].map(&:to_s)`).
BlockArg    <~~ push({blkT: makeBlockArg(pop())}) ~~>
            = "&" Expression
            | "&" !"&" <~~ push(makeVarRef("__fwd_b")) ~~> ;

// ->(a, b = 1) { ... } / -> { ... }: a lambda literal. Unlike a block it is a VALUE,
// and a `return` inside it returns from the lambda itself.
LambdaLit   <~~ push(makeLambda(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "->" [ "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" | Param { "," Param } ] LambdaBody ;
LambdaBody  = KwDo Body End | "{" [ BlockParams ] Body "}" ;

// A block passed to a method call: a real closure over the current scope.
Block       <~~ push(makeBlock(takeAll())) ~~>
            = NoLoopDo KwDo [ BlockParams ] GuardedBlockBody End
            | NoLambdaParam "{" [ BlockParams ] BlockBody "}" ;
// A `{` may not open a BLOCK inside a parenthesis-less lambda parameter list: in
// `-> a=a() { a }` the braces are the lambda's BODY, and without this guard the default
// value `a()` takes them as its block and the lambda is left with none. Like NoLoopDo
// above, the guard re-derives the answer by walking backwards - here to the `->` that
// opened the parameter list, stopping at anything that ends one.
NoLambdaParam = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var no = abnf.newToken("\x01lambdaparam", 0)
        var i = -1
        var depth = 0
        var steps = 0
        while (steps < 100000) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { return undefined }
            if (cc === 41 || cc === 93) { depth = depth + 1; i = i - 1; continue }
            if (cc === 40 || cc === 91) {
                if (depth === 0) { return undefined }
                depth = depth - 1
                i = i - 1
                continue
            }
            if (depth > 0) { i = i - 1; continue }
            if (cc === 123 || cc === 125 || cc === 59 || cc === 10) { return undefined }
            // A `->` at bracket depth zero: the parameter list it opened is what we are
            // standing in, unless it was parenthesised (`-> (a) { ... }`).
            if (cc === 62 && c.peek(i - 1) === 45) {
                var j = i + 1
                var dd = c.peek(j)
                var st2 = 0
                while (st2 < 1000 && (dd === 32 || dd === 9)) { j = j + 1; dd = c.peek(j); st2 = st2 + 1 }
                if (dd === 40) { return undefined }
                return no
            }
            i = i - 1
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;

// A `do` block may not open inside the CONDITION of a loop: `while flag do ... end`
// gives the `do` to the loop, not to a call of `flag`. A guard cannot carry a flag
// (guards must be pure functions of source and position), so it re-derives the answer
// by walking BACKWARDS to the start of the enclosing statement and reading its first
// word - the same shape as Swift's NoTrailCtx in languages/swift-*.abnf.
NoLoopDo = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var no = abnf.newToken("\x01loopdo", 0)
        var i = -1
        var depth = 0
        var steps = 0
        var start = 0
        var found = false
        while (steps < 100000 && !found) {
            steps = steps + 1
            var cc = c.peek(i)
            if (cc < 0) { start = i + 1; found = true; break }
            if (cc === 41 || cc === 93) { depth = depth + 1; i = i - 1; continue }
            if (cc === 40 || cc === 91) {
                // An unmatched opener starts the enclosing expression, so the word right
                // after it is the one to read: `defined?(while x do y end)` has its loop
                // header inside the parentheses.
                if (depth === 0) { start = i + 1; found = true; break }
                depth = depth - 1
                i = i - 1
                continue
            }
            if (depth > 0) { i = i - 1; continue }
            if (cc === 125 || cc === 123 || cc === 59) { start = i + 1; found = true; break }
            if (cc === 10) { start = i + 1; found = true; break }
            i = i - 1
        }
        if (!found) { return undefined }
        // Read the first word of that statement, skipping leading blanks.
        var j = start
        var dd = c.peek(j)
        var st2 = 0
        while (st2 < 1000 && (dd === 32 || dd === 9 || dd === 13)) { j = j + 1; dd = c.peek(j); st2 = st2 + 1 }
        var w = ""
        var st3 = 0
        while (st3 < 100 && ((dd >= 65 && dd <= 90) || (dd >= 97 && dd <= 122) || dd === 95)) {
            w = w + String.fromCharCode(dd)
            j = j + 1
            dd = c.peek(j)
            st3 = st3 + 1
        }
        if (w === "while" || w === "until" || w === "for") { return no }
        return undefined
    })()
